{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And the prince of the tribe of the children of Gad, Elasaph the son of Reuel.</b> With a resh and not with a dalet, for so it is written in the Masorah on this: לית כוותיה בכל שבטיא — 'there is none like it among all the tribes' — and one other (Numbers 7) בן רעואל המדיני. If so, the verse that mentioned at the beginning of the book for Gad 'Elasaph the son of Deuel,' with a dalet — He mentioned upon this לית כוותיה בכל השבטים. And he is called once with a dalet and once with a resh, and it is all one meaning, for the meaning is that he desired (רצה) El and knew Him."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The Masoretic note on Numbers 1:14 reads לית כוותיה בכל התורה for the name בן דעואל (with dalet); the note on Numbers 2:14 (with resh) reads לית כוותיה בכל שבטיא. R. Bahya appears to be correlating the two spellings with their respective Masoretic notes; the sentence beginning מאחר שהזכיר is textually awkward and rendered hyper-literally. The second citation, בן רעואל המדיני, follows Numbers 7:42 where the offering prince of Gad is אליסף בן דעואל; the exact referent of המדיני is unclear."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:29:46.608445492Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}